---
import GuideLayout from '@layouts/GuideLayout.astro';
import ReferenceBlock from '@components/ReferenceBlock.astro';
import { config } from './config';
import { toolPath, categoryPath } from '@lib/paths';
import { toolPathBySlug } from '@lib/tools/tool-link';
import { categories } from '@data/categories';
import type { EcosystemEntry } from '@data/types';

interface Props {
  title: string;
  description: string;
  readMinutes: number;
  updatedAt: string;
  entry: EcosystemEntry;
}

const props = Astro.props;
const category = categories.find(c => c.slug === config.categorySlug)!;
const toolHref = toolPath({ slug: config.slug, segment: category.segment });
const categoryHref = categoryPath(category);
---

<GuideLayout
  {...props}
  toolSlug={config.slug}
  toolName={config.name}
  toolCategorySlug={config.categorySlug}
>
  <section id="quick-answer">
    <h2>What is tic tac toe two player on one phone? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Tic tac toe two player is a 3 by 3 board for two people.
      X starts, and three in a row wins.
    </p>
    <p>
      You play tic tac toe online on this page by passing one phone.
      That is how you play tic tac toe same device, and tic tac toe no download means the page is the whole game.
    </p>
    <p>
      A search for tic tac toe two player online no account lands on this board.
      Xs and Os names the same two marks.
    </p>
    <a href={toolHref} class="cta-link">Open Tic Tac Toe</a>
  </section>

  <section id="why-one-device">
    <h2>Why does one phone matter? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      One phone matters because both players are already in the room.
      A hot seat keeps the match there instead of opening a second device.
    </p>
    <p>
      Passing one phone across a table when nobody has paper is the ordinary case.
      The purpose is a finished game with a score you can read out loud.
    </p>
    <p>
      Who goes first is a separate question.
      A <a href={toolPathBySlug('coin-flipper')}>coin flip</a> settles it, and a
      <a href={toolPathBySlug('dice-roller')}>dice roll</a> does the same job when a coin is the wrong call.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="how-a-turn-works">
    <h2>How does a turn work? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The banner names the seat that plays next.
      Tap an empty square and that seat's mark stays.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, X takes the top left and O takes the middle left.
      X then takes the top middle, and O takes the center.
      X takes the top right, so the top row is three X marks.
    </p>
    <p>The banner says X wins, and further taps do nothing.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rules">
    <h2>What are the noughts and crosses rules? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The noughts and crosses rules are this same 3 by 3 game.
      A diagonal counts as three in a row, the same as a row or a column.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>X places first, then O answers</li>
      <li>A mark stays where it landed</li>
      <li>Three matching marks in a line end the game</li>
      <li>Nine marks with no line is a draw</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      For example, X on the top left plus the center plus the bottom right is a diagonal win.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="hot-seat">
    <h2>How is a hot seat different from online matchmaking? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      A hot seat is two people sharing one phone.
      Online matchmaking looks for a stranger on another device.
    </p>
    <p>
      You pass the phone instead of waiting in a lobby.
      Unlike matchmaking, the empty seat is not a computer opponent.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="key-idea" heading="The other seat is a person">
      <p>The other seat is a person, so the page never moves for them.</p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Expecting the other seat to move by itself is the wrong picture of this page.
      This board lives in the <a href={categoryHref}>games category</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="filled-square">
    <h2>What happens if you tap a filled square? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      The second tap does nothing, and the turn stays put.
      The note under the grid says that square already has a mark.
    </p>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Tapping a filled square">
      <p>
        Tapping a filled square again and thinking the game ignored you is the common mistake.
        Do not tap it again and expect a new mark.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <ReferenceBlock type="common-mistake" heading="Calling a diagonal a draw">
      <p>
        Missing a diagonal and calling a finished line a draw comes from scanning only the rows.
        Check both diagonals before you call the game over.
      </p>
    </ReferenceBlock>
    <p>
      Avoid refreshing mid-match and expecting the score to still be there.
      Nothing is saved, so a refresh clears the tally and the grid.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section id="undo">
    <h2>Does undo remove the point? <span class="gold-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span></h2>
    <p>
      Undo takes back one move, and the score follows that move.
      Undo of a winning line takes that point off the tally.
    </p>
    <p>
      For example, the top-row win above reads X 1.
      Undo returns the score to X 0 and gives the turn back to X.
    </p>
    <p>
      Rematch clears the nine squares and hands the first move to the seat that went second.
      However, the wins and draws stay on the tally until you press Reset score or leave the page.
    </p>
  </section>
</GuideLayout>

<style>
  .cta-link {
    display: inline-flex;
    align-items: center;
    height: var(--touch-target);
    padding-inline: var(--space-5);
    margin-top: var(--space-5);
    background: var(--color-accent);
    color: var(--color-text-inverse);
    font-weight: var(--font-weight-semibold);
    font-size: var(--text-base);
    border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    text-decoration: none;
    transition: var(--transition-color);
  }
  .cta-link:hover { opacity: 0.9; text-decoration: none; }
</style>
